      "commentary": "Death returns and does something a lesser power would never do: he apologizes. Three nights this brahmin boy has waited in his house, honored yet unfed — a lapse in the sacred duty of hospitality that even the lord of death must repair. \"Homage to you,\" he says, and offers three boons, one for each unfed night. Notice the reversal of every expectation. The most fearsome being in existence bows to a child and admits fault. The Upanishad is quietly teaching that even Death is bound by dharma, by the moral order — no one, however powerful, stands above the law of right conduct. And this act of restitution is what opens the door: because Death owes Nachiketa, Nachiketa can ask Death the one question mortals are never permitted to have answered."
